      "updated": 1778923188797,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "[?] What is that? What is it used for?\nNamespace provides a logical organizing way for codes. While \"file\n\n[?] Is namespace concept similar in both C++ and Rust?\nIn Rust, there is nothing called namespace. In Rust, code separation and organization\nis handled directly by directory hierarchy.\n\nBefore jumping into the details, let's come up with a definition about file/module in Rust.\n\nIn Rust, file or module refer to the same thing. Each file (module) handles a separate logic.\n\nTherefore, if you want to group these logics under the umbrella of the bigger and more general logic,\nyou put the files under a directory. This directory now becomes a module.\n\nFrom the definition about, it also leads to another consequence that a module cannot have multiple definitions\nof the code which scatter across multiple .rs file.\n\n\n\nWhen it comes to C++ world, things start to change.\n\nIn C++, we don't have Module concept but instead Namespace, and File.\n\nFile represents a physical code groupping (just like Rust) whereas Namespace is an attempt\nto logically group code in wider span.\n\nTwo crucial differences between C++ and Rust are that C++ compiler doesn't really care about\nthe directory structure of the code and code living under the same Namespace can spread across\nmultiple .cpp file.\n\nIn C++, #include preprocessor is just a copy-paste code operation, i.e. when you #include <somethin.h>, Preprocessing step\nmerely looks for the specified file, then copies and pastes the entire code into the current file. Therefore, code placement\nisn't mattered that much, compiler just gets into the file that you specify in #include.\n\nRegarding the 2nd point, in C++, 2 separate files could add function definition into a namespace.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "[?] What is that? What is it used for?\nNamespace provides a logical organizing way for codes. While \"file\n\n[?] Is namespace concept similar in both C++ and Rust?\nIn Rust, there is nothing called namespace. In Rust, code separation and organization\nis handled directly by directory hierarchy.\n\nBefore jumping into the details, let's come up with a definition about file/module in Rust.\n\nIn Rust, file or module refer to the same thing. Each file (module) handles a separate logic.\n\nTherefore, if you want to group these logics under the umbrella of the bigger and more general logic,\nyou put the files under a directory. This directory now becomes a module.\n\nFrom the definition about, it also leads to another consequence that a module cannot have multiple definitions\nof the code which scatter across multiple .rs file.\n\n\n\nWhen it comes to C++ world, things start to change.\n\nIn C++, we don't have Module concept but instead Namespace, and File.\n\nFile represents a physical code groupping (just like Rust) whereas Namespace is an attempt\nto logically group code in wider span.\n\nTwo crucial differences between C++ and Rust are that C++ compiler doesn't really care about\nthe directory structure of the code and code living under the same Namespace can spread across\nmultiple .cpp file.\n\nIn C++, #include preprocessor is just a copy-paste code operation, i.e. when you #include <somethin.h>, Preprocessing step\nmerely looks for the specified file, then copies and pastes the entire code into the current file. Therefore, code placement\nisn't mattered that much, compiler just gets into the file that you specify in #include.\n\nRegarding the 2nd point, in C++, 2 separate files could add function definition into a namespace.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
